/// RESP request/reply over a pair of value streams, independent of the transport carrying them.
///
/// A transport and a connection are wired together with their streams: take the replies the
/// transport receives, establish the connection with them, hand the requests the connection
/// returns to the transport to send, then await the connection. See `tcp.connect` for a transport
/// that does this for its caller.
///
/// The connection is the mechanics of talking to a server: sending commands and pairing replies
/// with them, subscriptions, push messages, and transactions. Higher level, typed operations are
/// functions in the `commands` interface that borrow the connection.
interface connection {
    use types.{connect-opts, duration-milliseconds, error, resp-value, string-or-bytes, subscription-event, subscription-target};

    @since(version = 0.3.0-dev)
    resource connection {
        /// Establishes a connection over a transport.
        ///
        /// `replies` are the values the transport receives from the server, and `closed` resolves
        /// when the transport closes, to `ok` for a graceful close or to the error otherwise, see
        /// `tcp.transport.receive`. Returns the requests for the transport to write to the server,
        /// in order, and the connection once it is established.
        ///
        /// The connection sends `HELLO 3`, with the `auth` and `client-name` of `opts`, as its first
        /// command. This pins the connection to RESP3 and confirms the server can be reached. The
        /// future resolves to the connection once HELLO succeeds, or to the error that stopped it.
        /// The rest of `opts` is applied before the connection is returned.
        ///
        /// It then reports the library to the server, `CLIENT SETINFO LIB-NAME componentized:valkey`
        /// and `LIB-VER` with the version of the package, ignoring an error from a server that does
        /// not know CLIENT SETINFO. They survive `reset`, `client-setinfo` replaces them.
        ///
        /// Async so the connection keeps working between commands after it returns, e.g. to read
        /// push messages and to send keep-alive pings.
        new: static async func(replies: stream<resp-value>, closed: future<result<_, error>>, opts: option<connect-opts>) -> tuple<stream<resp-value>, future<result<connection, error>>>;

        /// The server's properties from the reply to HELLO, e.g. `server`, `version` and `mode`.
        server-properties: func() -> list<tuple<string, resp-value>>;

        /// Sends a command and waits for its reply.
        ///
        /// Several commands may be in flight at once, replies are paired with commands in the order
        /// the commands were sent.
        ///
        /// For SUBSCRIBE, PSUBSCRIBE, SSUBSCRIBE and their UNSUBSCRIBE counterparts, prefer a
        /// `subscription`, which owns its targets' messages. Once the connection has one, it alone
        /// sends these commands, and they fail with a client error before anything is sent, here,
        /// with `exchange-all`, `emit` and `transaction`.
        ///
        /// RESET, HELLO and CLIENT REPLY always fail with a client error. See `reset` and
        /// `server-properties`, CLIENT REPLY would stop replies from pairing with commands, see
        /// `emit`.
        ///
        /// A command has at least one word, an empty command fails with a client error.
        exchange: async func(command: list<string-or-bytes>) -> result<resp-value, error>;

        /// Sends the commands one after another, with no other command sent between them, and waits
        /// for every reply.
        ///
        /// Each command has its own result, in order, as if each was sent with `exchange`. Commands
        /// sharing a connection may otherwise be interleaved with other callers' commands, which
        /// matters for a sequence that must not be interrupted, e.g. MULTI, its commands and EXEC.
        exchange-all: async func(commands: list<list<string-or-bytes>>) -> list<result<resp-value, error>>;

        /// Sends a command without waiting for its reply, resolving once the command is written.
        ///
        /// The server still replies, the reply is discarded when it arrives and an error it reports
        /// is not returned. An implementation may instead ask the server not to reply, e.g. with
        /// `CLIENT REPLY SKIP`.
        ///
        /// A command has at least one word, an empty command fails with a client error.
        emit: async func(command: list<string-or-bytes>) -> result<_, error>;

        /// Marks the keys to watch, EXEC aborts the next transaction when any of them changed.
        ///
        /// Watched keys belong to the connection, and EXEC, DISCARD or UNWATCH from any caller sharing
        /// it clears them. Use a connection of your own for WATCH, the reads it guards, and the
        /// transaction.
        ///
        /// WATCH key [ key ... ]
        /// https://valkey.io/commands/watch/
        ///
        /// At least one key, no keys fails with a client error.
        watch: async func(keys: list<string>) -> result<_, error>;

        /// Forgets all the watched keys.
        ///
        /// UNWATCH
        /// https://valkey.io/commands/unwatch/
        unwatch: async func() -> result<_, error>;

        /// Runs the commands as a transaction, MULTI, each command, then EXEC, with no other command
        /// sent between them, see `exchange-all`.
        ///
        /// Returns none when a watched key changed and the transaction was aborted, otherwise the
        /// reply of each command in order. A command that fails while running is an error value in
        /// its place, the others still ran. A command rejected while queuing, e.g. an unknown
        /// command, fails the transaction with that error, none of the commands ran.
        ///
        /// MULTI and EXEC are only sent as part of a transaction, the commands between them reply
        /// QUEUED rather than their reply.
        ///
        /// MULTI, EXEC
        /// https://valkey.io/commands/multi/
        /// https://valkey.io/commands/exec/
        transaction: async func(commands: list<list<string-or-bytes>>) -> result<option<list<resp-value>>, error>;

        /// A new subscription, subscribed to nothing yet, see the subscription's `subscribe`.
        ///
        /// From the first subscription, the connection owns its subscriptions: SUBSCRIBE,
        /// PSUBSCRIBE, SSUBSCRIBE and their UNSUBSCRIBE counterparts sent with `exchange` and the like
        /// fail with a client error, so the server's subscriptions stay in step with the
        /// connection's. Subscriptions made with `exchange` before are unsupported. They may keep
        /// delivering to `dead-letters`, the exact behavior is undocumented.
        ///
        /// Fails with a client error when the connection component was built without pub/sub,
        /// e.g. `connection-minimal`. Its push messages are then all dead letters, and SUBSCRIBE and
        /// the like may still be sent with `exchange`.
        subscription: func() -> result<subscription, error>;

        /// The values nothing else claims, kept apart from replies.
        ///
        /// Push messages that no subscription owns, e.g. client tracking invalidations, messages
        /// and confirmations of SUBSCRIBE sent with `exchange`, or kinds of push messages from a
        /// newer server. Values the server sends unprompted, when no command is waiting for a
        /// reply, are included, e.g. the feed of MONITOR, which are not RESP push values.
        ///
        /// Each call returns a new stream that receives every value arriving after the call,
        /// values arriving while no stream is open are dropped. A stream that falls too far behind
        /// is closed rather than holding up replies, the values it missed are lost.
        dead-letters: func() -> stream<resp-value>;

        /// Resets the connection to the state of a new one, then repeats the handshake of `new`.
        ///
        /// RESET discards a transaction in progress, forgets the watched keys, unsubscribes from
        /// every channel, turns off client tracking and the like, selects database 0, and forgets
        /// the client name and the authenticated user. It also switches the connection to RESP2,
        /// so it is followed at once by `HELLO 3` with the `auth` and `client-name` the connection
        /// was established with, with no other command sent between them. `server-properties`
        /// then holds the new reply to HELLO.
        ///
        /// The subscriptions are unsubscribed from every target, each with the reason `reset`, and
        /// stay open for new targets.
        ///
        /// No other command is sent until the handshake succeeds, so none runs on the connection
        /// RESET leaves. When the handshake fails, e.g. the credentials expired since the connection
        /// was established, the connection closes with its error, and the commands queued after
        /// the reset fail with it without being sent. Establish a new connection.
        ///
        /// RESET
        /// https://valkey.io/commands/reset/
        reset: async func() -> result<_, error>;

        /// Sends PING when no command has been sent and no reply is outstanding for `interval`,
        /// `none` disables it. A PING that fails closes the connection. Disabled by default.
        ///
        /// Unlike TCP keep-alive, a PING counts as activity for the server's idle client timeout,
        /// and works over any transport.
        set-keep-alive: func(interval: option<duration-milliseconds>);
    }

    /// The messages of a set of channels, patterns of channels and shard channels, that changes
    /// as targets are subscribed to and unsubscribed from, see `connection.subscription`.
    ///
    /// Subscriptions sharing a connection may overlap, the server stays subscribed to a target while
    /// any subscription has it. The subscription stays open until it is dropped, with or without
    /// targets or streams, then it unsubscribes from its targets. It keeps its connection open.
    @since(version = 0.3.0-dev)
    resource subscription {
        /// Subscribes to the targets, resolving once the server confirms each of them, see
        /// `subscription-event.subscribed`. Targets already subscribed to are left as they are. The
        /// server unsubscribes from a shard channel on its own when its slot moves to another node.
        ///
        /// Each kind of target is subscribed to with its own command, queued with no other command
        /// between them. Every command is sent, even when another fails, and those that succeed
        /// take effect, see `subscription-event.subscribed` and `targets`. The error then holds the
        /// error of each command that failed, e.g. `PSUBSCRIBE: NOPERM ...`. A command is accepted
        /// or refused as a whole, targets of one kind are subscribed to all or none.
        ///
        /// SUBSCRIBE channel [ channel ... ]
        /// PSUBSCRIBE pattern [ pattern ... ]
        /// SSUBSCRIBE shardchannel [ shardchannel ... ]
        /// https://valkey.io/commands/subscribe/
        /// https://valkey.io/commands/psubscribe/
        /// https://valkey.io/commands/ssubscribe/
        ///
        /// At least one target, no targets fails with a client error.
        subscribe: async func(targets: list<subscription-target>) -> result<_, error>;

        /// The events arriving after the call: targets subscribed to and unsubscribed from, and
        /// messages, in the order they happened.
        ///
        /// Each call returns a new stream, every open stream receives every event. Events arriving
        /// while no stream is open are kept for the next stream, up to 256, later ones are dropped.
        /// Dropping a stream does not unsubscribe, call `events` again for another.
        ///
        /// Pub/sub delivers each message at most once, to all listeners. A stream receives
        /// no events from before it opened, beyond those kept while no stream was open, and does
        /// not start with the current targets, see `targets`. Messages published while nothing
        /// listens are lost, e.g. before a target is subscribed to, after the connection closed, or
        /// past what a lagging stream holds. For delivery that is guaranteed, use a Valkey stream,
        /// see XADD and XREADGROUP.
        ///
        /// The stream closes once the subscription is dropped, the future then resolves to `ok`,
        /// or to the error that closed the connection. A stream that falls too far behind is
        /// closed rather than holding up the connection, its future resolves to `lagged`, and the
        /// events it missed, messages and changes of targets alike, are lost. The future resolves
        /// after the stream delivered what it holds, read the stream to its end first.
        events: func() -> tuple<stream<subscription-event>, future<result<_, error>>>;

        /// The targets the server confirmed, in the order they were subscribed to, until they are
        /// unsubscribed from.
        ///
        /// Once the connection closes, the targets confirmed until then are still listed, e.g. to
        /// subscribe to them on a new connection. `subscribe` and `unsubscribe` then fail with the
        /// error that closed the connection, and leave the targets as they are.
        targets: func() -> list<subscription-target>;

        /// Unsubscribes from the targets, none for every target, resolving once each is
        /// unsubscribed from, see `subscription-event.unsubscribed`. Targets not subscribed to are
        /// left as they are. The server stays subscribed to targets other subscriptions have.
        ///
        /// Each kind of target is unsubscribed from with its own command, as for `subscribe`, e.g.
        /// the `targets` may be given as they are.
        ///
        /// UNSUBSCRIBE [ channel [ channel ... ] ]
        /// PUNSUBSCRIBE [ pattern [ pattern ... ] ]
        /// SUNSUBSCRIBE [ shardchannel [ shardchannel ... ] ]
        /// https://valkey.io/commands/unsubscribe/
        /// https://valkey.io/commands/punsubscribe/
        /// https://valkey.io/commands/sunsubscribe/
        ///
        /// A list of targets has at least one target, otherwise fails with a client error. None
        /// unsubscribes from every target.
        unsubscribe: async func(targets: option<list<subscription-target>>) -> result<_, error>;
    }
}
